.Dd $Mdocdate: September 1 2026 $
.Dt XSUNABA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Xsunaba
.Nd run X11 applications in a nested Xephyr display under dedicated accounts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -amnesiac
.Op Ar command Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility runs X11 GUI applications on
.Ox
inside a nested
.Xr Xephyr 1
X server, under dedicated local accounts, with optional filesystem
restriction via
.Xr unveil 2 .
.Pp
The isolation boundary is Unix account separation combined with X11
authentication and Unix socket permissions:
.Bl -enum -compact -offset indent
.It
The application runs under a dedicated account, never as the invoking
user: the persistent
.Sy _xsunaba_app
account, or, in amnesiac mode, a fresh
.Sy _xsunaba_ Ns Ao Ar random Ac
account created for the session.
.It
.Xr Xephyr 1
runs as the dedicated
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr
account, never as the application account.
.It
Each invocation generates a fresh 128-bit MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 cookie,
stored in separate mode-0600 authority files for the Xephyr server
and the application.
The invoking user cannot read them.
.It
The nested Unix socket
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix/X Ns Ao Ar NN Ac
is tightened to mode 0660, owned by
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr
and a group restricted to the application account, so only the two
sandbox accounts can connect.
.It
The application environment is rebuilt from scratch:
.Ev DISPLAY
points to the nested display,
.Ev XAUTHORITY
to its own authority file, and no parent-display credentials or host
IPC variables are forwarded.
.It
.Xr unveil 2
and
.Xr pledge 2
are applied on top as defense in depth.
.El
.Pp
The Xephyr outer window is a normal host-WM-resizable window.
.Ev WIDTH
and
.Ev HEIGHT
set its initial size only; resizing it afterwards resizes the nested
screen through Xephyr's RandR support
.Pq ephyrResizeScreen ,
so applications inside observe the new geometry.
.Pp
When invoked from the command line,
.Nm
validates its arguments and executes the root-owned helper
.Pa /usr/local/libexec/xsunaba-helper
through
.Xr doas 1 ,
which creates the session, starts Xephyr, launches the application,
waits for it to exit, stops Xephyr and cleans up all runtime state.
.Pp
Xephyr is itself an X client of the parent X server: a malicious
client already authorized on the parent display may still observe,
resize, inject input into or otherwise interfere with the Xephyr
outer window.
.Nm
prevents direct access to the nested X server and prevents the nested
application from reaching the parent display; it cannot isolate two
clients of the same parent X server.
Stronger host-to-sandbox isolation requires a separate X
server/session/VT, a virtual machine, or another display
architecture.
.Pp
Crossing the Unix-user isolation boundary should require exploiting
an additional trusted/privileged interface or a privilege-boundary
vulnerability, rather than merely escaping pledge/unveil.
Globally accessible resources (world-readable files, world-writable
directories, services with unauthenticated sockets, shared groups,
inherited file descriptors) are outside that boundary.
.Sh AMNESIAC MODE
.Fl -amnesiac
creates a brand-new Unix user for this invocation only:
.Bl -enum -compact -offset indent
.It
A fresh account
.Sy _xsunaba_ Ns Ao Ar random Ac
with a private primary group, no usable password,
.Pa /sbin/nologin
shell and a private home
.Pq Pa /var/xsunaba/home/_xsunaba_ Ns Ao Ar random Ac
mode 0700.
.It
The helper sets
.Xr umask 2
to 077 before the application starts, so application-created files
default to 0600 and directories to 0700.
The application may deliberately change permissions on objects it
owns; the umask is a default, not an access-control policy.
.It
The application receives credentials only for the nested display; the
nested socket is group-owned by the ephemeral private group.
.It
When the session ends (normal exit, Xephyr failure, startup failure,
SIGINT or SIGTERM), remaining processes under the ephemeral UID are
terminated, the session directory is removed, the verified ephemeral
home is removed, and the account and its private group are deleted.
Nothing persists between invocations.
.It
Interrupted sessions are recovered conservatively by the next
amnesiac invocation: only session directories holding the helper's
liveness lock and naming the account in their own account file are
considered, only once no process remains under that UID.
Unrelated accounts are never touched.
.El
.Pp
Persistent mode (without the flag) keeps the existing
.Sy _xsunaba_app
account and its shared home.
.Sh PRIVILEGED HELPER
The helper is deliberately small and only reachable through a narrow
.Xr doas.conf 5
rule:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
permit nopass USER as root cmd /usr/local/libexec/xsunaba-helper \e
        args --parent-display
.Ed
.Pp
The rule permits only the helper executable, as root, and only
invocations starting with
.Fl -parent-display .
It grants nothing else.
The caller cannot supply an arbitrary Xauthority file for the nested
display, an existing DISPLAY to join, a UID, a session directory, a
socket pathname or other values that would permit joining another
session.
The helper:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
validates every argument and fails closed;
.It
requires the parent Xauthority to be owned by the invoking user;
.It
allocates the nested display under a lock and never joins an
existing session;
.It
copies only the parent-display MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 records from the
invoking user's Xauthority into a file readable only by
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr ;
.It
generates the nested cookie from
.Pa /dev/urandom
and writes it only into the two mode-0600 authority files;
.It
drops privileges with verified
.Fn setgid
and
.Fn setuid
sequences, resets supplementary groups and checks that privileges
cannot be regained;
.It
restricts the nested socket to 0660 owned by
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr
plus the application socket group once Xephyr has created it;
.It
runs Xephyr with
.Fl noreset
so the server cannot recreate the socket with looser permissions
after a reset, with
.Fl resizeable
so the host WM may resize the window, and with
.Fl no-host-grab
so Xephyr performs no manual keyboard/mouse host grab
.Pq see Sx INPUT HANDLING ;
.It
sets
.Xr umask 2
to 077 before creating session data;
.It
in amnesiac mode creates the ephemeral account with
.Xr useradd 8
.Pq no password, nologin shell, private group
and deletes it with
.Xr userdel 8
plus
.Xr groupdel 8
after terminating every process under its UID;
.It
rebuilds the application environment and optionally applies
.Xr unveil 2 ;
.It
cleans up the socket and the session directory on exit;
.It
finally calls
.Xr pledge 2
with
.Ql stdio rpath cpath fattr proc exec getpw
for the teardown phase.
.El
.Sh INPUT HANDLING
Xephyr's input path is a plain pass-through: host motion, button and
key events become nested events.
Its only input manipulation by default is a manual keyboard/pointer
grab on the host display triggered by the ctrl+shift key combination
.Pq ephyrProcessKeyRelease ,
which changes the window title and can leave the mouse stuck inside
the window.
Browser shortcuts such as ctrl+shift+U (Tor Browser's circuit
display) engage exactly this state machine right before popup menus
are used.
.Nm
starts Xephyr with
.Fl no-host-grab ,
which removes that manual grab: input stays plain pass-through, no
host grabs occur, and MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 authentication is
unaffected.
The former forced exact-fit browser geometry was removed for the same
reason.
These changes are source-verified; final confirmation of the Tor
Browser circuit-menu behavior requires the runtime checks on a real
.Ox
workstation
.Pq see Sx CAVEATS .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The invoking process must have
.Ev DISPLAY
set to a local display
.Pq e.g. Ql :0
and
.Ev HOME
set.
The parent X authority is taken from
.Ev XAUTHORITY
or, if unset,
.Pa $HOME/.Xauthority .
.Pp
The application's environment is rebuilt and contains only
.Ev PATH , HOME , USER , LOGNAME , SHELL , DISPLAY , XAUTHORITY ,
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
and, if present in the invoking environment,
.Ev TERM , TZ , LANG
and
.Ev LC_* .
Variables such as
.Ev DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS , SSH_AUTH_SOCK , SSH_AGENT_PID ,
.Ev GPG_AGENT_INFO , WAYLAND_DISPLAY , SESSION_MANAGER ,
.Ev XDG_SESSION_* , XDG_CONFIG_HOME , XDG_DATA_HOME ,
.Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME , KRB5CCNAME ,
sndio variables and
.Ev XSUNABA_*
are never forwarded.
.Bl -tag -width "XSUNABA_UNVEIL"
.It Ev XSUNABA_UNVEIL
Comma-separated list of
.Ar path : Ar perm
entries passed to
.Xr unveil 2
for the application.
When unset, no unveil restrictions are applied; the account and
credential separation still holds.
.Pp
Permission codes are single-letter abbreviations that may be
combined:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "rwxc" -compact -offset indent
.It r
Read files, list directories.
.It rx
Read and execute.
.It rw
Read and write existing files.
.It rwx
Read, write, and execute existing files.
.It rwc
Read, write, and create new files.
.It rwxc
Read, write, execute, and create (full access).
.El
.Pp
After the user entries,
.Nm
additionally unveils, before locking:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" -compact -offset indent
.It Pa /tmp/.X11-unix/X Ns Ao Ar NN Ac
with
.Ql w
.Pq connect to the exact nested socket ;
the
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix
directory itself is never exposed.
.It the application authority file
with
.Ql r .
.It the per-session XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
with
.Ql rwxc .
.El
.It Ev XSUNABA_DISPLAY
Starting X display number for
.Xr Xephyr 1 .
If the socket
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix/X Ns Ao Ar N Ac
already exists,
the helper scans upward for a free display.
Default:
.Ql :32 .
.It Ev WIDTH
.Xr Xephyr 1
initial display width in pixels; the window is resizable afterwards.
Default:
.Ql 1024 .
.It Ev HEIGHT
.Xr Xephyr 1
initial display height in pixels; the window is resizable afterwards.
Default:
.Ql 768 .
.It Ev VERBOSE
When set to a non-empty value, emits informational messages.
Authentication material never appears in the output.
.El
.Pp
A non-empty legacy
.Ev XSUNABA_PLEDGE
value is rejected:
.Xr OpenBSD::Pledge 3p
does not expose the
.Fa execpromises
argument of
.Xr pledge 2 ,
so an executed program would not be restricted and any promise
silently fails to apply.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/run/xsunaba/XXXXXXXX.../xephyr/parent-auth"
.It Pa /usr/local/bin/Xsunaba
Frontend, run by the invoking user.
.It Pa /usr/local/libexec/xsunaba-helper
Root-owned privileged session helper, invoked through
.Xr doas 1 .
.It Pa /var/run/xsunaba/
Root-owned runtime directory (mode 0711), created by the helper.
.It Pa /var/run/xsunaba/ Ns Ar random Ns Pa /xephyr/
Session directory of the Xephyr account (mode 0700).
.It Pa .../xephyr/parent-auth
Parent-display MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 records for Xephyr (mode 0600,
owned by
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr ) .
.It Pa .../xephyr/server-auth
Nested-display server cookie for Xephyr
.Fl auth
(mode 0600, owned by
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr ) .
.It Pa .../app/
Session directory of the application account (mode 0700).
.It Pa .../app/client-auth
Nested-display client cookie (mode 0600, owned by
.Sy _xsunaba_app ) .
.It Pa .../app/run/
Per-session
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
(mode 0700).
.It Pa /var/run/xsunaba/ Ns Ar random Ns Pa /active.lock
Amnesiac liveness lock (mode 0600, root); stale sessions are reaped
when it is free.
.It Pa /var/run/xsunaba/ Ns Ar random Ns Pa /account
Amnesiac session's account name (mode 0600, root), used to verify
identity during reaping.
.It Pa /var/xsunaba/home/_xsunaba_ Ns Ar random Ns Pa /
Private amnesiac
.Ev HOME
(mode 0700, owned by the ephemeral account); removed at session end.
.It Pa /tmp/.X11-unix/X Ns Ao Ar NN Ac
Nested X11 socket, tightened to mode 0660
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr
plus the application socket group.
.It Pa /usr/X11R6/bin/Xephyr
The nested X server executable.
.El
.Pp
The invoking user owns none of the authority files and has no read
permission on them.
Nothing security-sensitive is stored in
.Pa /tmp .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
The exit status of the application is propagated (signal deaths are
reported as 128 plus the signal number).
.Sh EXAMPLES
Launch Firefox with filesystem restrictions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ XSUNABA_UNVEIL="/usr/local/bin/firefox:rx,/tmp:rwc,/etc:r,/dev:r,\e
  /home/_xsunaba_app/.mozilla:rwc" Xsunaba firefox
.Ed
.Pp
Run Tor Browser with a throwaway account:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ Xsunaba --amnesiac tor-browser
.Ed
.Pp
Run xterm with minimal access, verbose output enabled:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ VERBOSE=1 XSUNABA_UNVEIL="/usr/X11R6/bin/xterm:rx,/tmp:rwc,/etc:r" \e
  Xsunaba xterm
.Ed
.Pp
Launch a sandbox on a custom display with custom initial geometry:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ XSUNABA_DISPLAY=":40" WIDTH=1280 HEIGHT=900 Xsunaba gimp
.Ed
.Pp
Use Xsunaba as a Perl module:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/perl
require '/usr/local/bin/Xsunaba';

Xsunaba::launch(
    app      => '/usr/local/bin/firefox',
    args     => ['--private-window'],
    amnesiac => 1,
    unveil   => ['/usr/local/bin/firefox:rx', '/tmp:rwc'],
);
.Ed
.Pp
The low-level module API
.Fn pledge ,
.Fn unveil ,
.Fn unveil_lock
and
.Fn sandbox
restrict the current Perl process only; they perform no UID
transitions and start no Xephyr.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Set
.Ev VERBOSE
to a non-empty value to see informational messages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[INFO] using display :32
[INFO] session directory /var/run/xsunaba/<random>
[INFO] Xephyr started (PID 12345)
[INFO] launched 'firefox' (PID 12346)
[INFO] stopped Xephyr (PID 12345)
[INFO] cleanup complete
.Ed
.Pp
Errors (always printed) indicate fatal conditions such as a missing
or non-local parent
.Ev DISPLAY ,
an unreadable or unowned parent X authority,
missing sandbox accounts,
an invalid application path,
Xephyr failing to start,
or a failed privilege drop.
All such conditions fail closed and clean up the session.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr doas 1 ,
.Xr perl 1 ,
.Xr Xephyr 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr umask 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr doas.conf 5 ,
.Xr X 7 ,
.Xr groupadd 8 ,
.Xr groupdel 8 ,
.Xr sndiod 8 ,
.Xr useradd 8 ,
.Xr userdel 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
is based on a script by
.An Milosz Galazka
.Pq circa 2013
and was ported to
.Ox
and
.Xr doas 1
by
.An Morgan Aldridge
.Pq 2020 .
.An David Uhden Collado
rewrote it in Perl
.Pq 2025
and added unveil integration with Xephyr display isolation
.Pq 2026 .
The same-UID design (in which the login user could read the nested
cookie files and the application inherited the login UID) was
replaced in 2026 by dedicated
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr
and
.Sy _xsunaba_app
accounts, a narrow root helper, split authority files, socket
permission tightening, and rebuilt environments.
Later additions: a host-WM-resizable Xephyr window
.Pq Fl resizeable ,
amnesiac per-session accounts, and pass-through input handling
.Pq Fl no-host-grab
to address browser popup-menu selection problems.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Bl -bullet
.It
Xephyr is a client of the parent X server and is not a complete
security boundary; see
.Sx DESCRIPTION .
.It
Persistent-mode invocations share the sandbox accounts and are not
mutually isolated: an application in one session can read another
session's application-side authority file and shared home directory.
Amnesiac mode provides a fresh UID per session.
.It
The umask 077 applied by the helper is a default only; applications
can deliberately change permissions on files they own.
.It
The parent X socket must permit connections from the
.Sy _xsunaba_xephyr
account; the default mode 0777 does.
.It
The application cannot play audio through
.Xr sndiod 8
unless the administrator shares the sndio cookie with the sandbox
account.
.It
There is no window manager inside the Xephyr display; the
application starts at the initial resolution and follows Xephyr
window resizes through RandR.
.It
The input fixes
.Pq Fl no-host-grab ,
removal of forced browser geometry
are source-verified; whether they fully resolve the Tor Browser
circuit-menu selection problem requires the runtime checks below:
.Pp
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
start
.Nm
with xterm, Firefox and Tor Browser;
.It
open menus, context menus, dropdowns, the Tor circuit display
.Pq ctrl+shift+U
and click every item;
.It
resize the Xephyr window and repeat;
.It
compare against plain
.Xr Xephyr 1
with and without
.Fl no-host-grab
and against
.Pa tools/popup-grab-test
.Pq make tools .
.El
.It
.Nm
is a mitigation, not a guarantee; combine it with other
.Ox
security practices.
.El
